 :lesson/sections
 [{:section/type :communication-multiplied,
   :section/id 21,
   :section/title "They have to go",
   :section/body
   ["Tienen que ir You will remember that in Spanish the same verbs can be talking about you, he or she."
    "For example Tiene que ir You have to go Or Tiene que ir He/she has to go This also applies in the plural forms, a Spanish verb can be referring to you all or they."
    "For example Pueden ir You (all) can go/ they can go Van a ir You (all) are going to go/they are going to go Necesitan ir You (all) need to go/ they need to go If sentences are ambiguous you use a pronoun to identify who you are talking about, much like in English Él tiene que ir he has to go Ella tiene que ir she has to go Usted tiene que ir you have to go Now let´s examine the plural forms."
    "Tienen que ir You (all) have to go Or Tienen que ir They have to go If sentences are ambiguous, again you use a pronoun as an identifier."
    "Ellos tienen que ir They (men) have to go Ellas tienen que ir They (women) have to go Ustedes tienen que ir You (all) have to go If you are talking about a group of men and women you use ellos."
    "Again the best way to understand this lesson is to use the language. Practice understanding this concept in exercise 24A and then create your own Spanish with and without pronouns in exercise 24B."]}],
 :lesson/number 24,
 :lesson/intro ["No new words in this lesson just a new structure to round out your Spanish."],
 :lesson/step {:step/number 5, :step/title "Bringing it all home – talking about other people"},
